from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Cryptography''s three core goals are usually stated as…","options":["speed, size, and cost","confidentiality, integrity, and authenticity","compression, hashing, and encoding","backup, logging, and monitoring"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Keeping a message secret, unchanged, and provably from a known sender are the three pillars — see ''What cryptography protects''."},{"prompt":"In symmetric encryption, the sender and receiver…","options":["use different keys they never share","share one secret key used to both encrypt and decrypt","need no key at all","publish the key openly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Symmetric ciphers use a single shared key for both directions — see ''Symmetric encryption and stream ciphers''."},{"prompt":"The one-time pad computes ciphertext as…","options":["message plus key, ignoring length","message XOR a random key as long as the message","message times key mod 26","the hash of the message"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A one-time pad XORs the plaintext with a truly random key of equal length, used only once."},{"prompt":"A cryptographic hash function is designed to be…","options":["easy to reverse","collision-resistant and one-way","reversible with the key","the same as encryption"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Good hashes are one-way and make finding two inputs with the same output infeasible — see ''Message integrity''."},{"prompt":"Diffie-Hellman key exchange lets two parties…","options":["agree on a shared secret over a public channel","sign a document","compress a key","store passwords safely"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Two parties derive the same secret from public messages, with no shared key beforehand."},{"prompt":"In RSA, the public and private keys are related through…","options":["a shared password","the difficulty of factoring a large number","a lookup table","the sender''s IP address"],"answer":1,"explanation":"RSA''s security rests on the hardness of factoring n = p*q into its primes — see ''Public-key encryption''."},{"prompt":"A digital signature primarily provides…","options":["confidentiality","faster encryption","authenticity and integrity of a message","compression"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A signature proves who sent a message and that it was not altered."},{"prompt":"HTTPS is HTTP running over…","options":["a faster cable","the TLS protocol","a compression layer","a password file"],"answer":1,"explanation":"TLS wraps HTTP so the connection is encrypted and the server is authenticated — see ''How HTTPS works''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What cryptography protects: confidentiality, integrity, authenticity', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Name the three goals cryptography serves, tell them apart with concrete scenarios, and place a few historical ciphers on the map. Warm-up: if you encrypt a message but an attacker flips some bits in transit, which goal did encryption alone fail to protect?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Cryptography turns messages into forms that resist a determined adversary. Three goals recur. Confidentiality means an eavesdropper learns nothing about the content. Integrity means any tampering is detected — the receiver can tell whether a single bit changed. Authenticity means the receiver can verify who produced the message. These are distinct: a message can be secret yet tampered with, or unaltered yet forged. A central lesson, often called Kerckhoffs''s principle, is that security must rest on the secrecy of the key alone, never on hiding the algorithm. We assume the attacker knows the design and still cannot win."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"The Caesar cipher shifts each letter by a fixed amount: with shift 3, HELLO becomes KHOOR. It offers weak confidentiality and no integrity — and with only 25 shifts, you can break it by hand. A substitution cipher maps each letter to another; it has a huge key space but falls to frequency analysis, since ''e'' stays the most common symbol. The Vigenere cipher uses a repeating keyword to vary the shift, resisting simple frequency counts until the key length is guessed. None of these classical schemes provides integrity or authenticity: an attacker can still alter ciphertext, and the receiver cannot prove the sender. Modern cryptography addresses all three goals with separate, well-analyzed tools."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Encrypt CAB with a Caesar shift of 1. 2) Which goal is violated if an attacker reads your message but changes nothing? 3) Which goal is violated if the message arrives unchanged but was actually written by an impostor? 4) Why is a 25-key cipher insecure even though the key is ''secret''? 5) State Kerckhoffs''s principle in one line. Answers: 1) DBC. 2) Confidentiality. 3) Authenticity. 4) The key space is tiny; an attacker simply tries all 25 shifts. 5) Security should depend only on the secret key, not on keeping the algorithm hidden."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming encryption automatically guarantees integrity — it does not, which is why we later add MACs. Mistake: trusting a secret algorithm; secrecy of design tends to fail once the code leaks. Exit: (a) Which goal ensures tampering is detectable? (b) Should security depend on hiding the algorithm? Answers: integrity; no, only on the secret key."}]}'::jsonb, 'Encryption hides content; it does not by itself prove who sent a message or that nobody changed it. Those are separate goals with separate tools.', 12),
  (3, 'Symmetric encryption and stream ciphers', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how a shared key encrypts and decrypts, work through XOR by hand, and describe how a stream cipher stretches a short key into a long keystream. Warm-up: what is 1 XOR 1, and what is 1 XOR 0?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"In symmetric encryption one secret key, held by both parties, encrypts and decrypts. The simplest perfectly secret scheme is the one-time pad: XOR the plaintext with a truly random key of the same length. It is unbreakable in theory, but the key is as long as the message and must never be reused — reuse leaks (c1 XOR c2) = (m1 XOR m2), exposing both messages. A stream cipher makes the pad practical: a short key seeds a pseudorandom generator that outputs a long keystream, which is XORed with the plaintext. Security now depends on the keystream being indistinguishable from random. The same reuse rule applies: never encrypt two messages with the same keystream, which is why a fresh nonce accompanies the key."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"XOR bit by bit: encrypt m = 1011 with key k = 0110. Then c = m XOR k = 1101. To recover m, compute c XOR k = 1101 XOR 0110 = 1011 — the original. This shows XOR is its own inverse. Keystream reuse danger: suppose two messages m1 and m2 are each XORed with the same keystream z, giving c1 and c2. An attacker computes c1 XOR c2 = (m1 XOR z) XOR (m2 XOR z) = m1 XOR m2, canceling the keystream entirely and leaking the XOR of the plaintexts — often enough to read both. Modern stream ciphers such as ChaCha20 take a key plus a unique nonce so every message gets a distinct keystream, sidestepping this trap."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Compute 1100 XOR 1010. 2) Decrypt c = 0111 that was encrypted with key 0011. 3) Why must a one-time pad key never repeat? 4) What does an attacker learn from c1 XOR c2 under keystream reuse? 5) What extra input lets one key safely encrypt many messages? Answers: 1) 0110. 2) c XOR k = 0111 XOR 0011 = 0100. 3) Reuse leaks the XOR of the two plaintexts, breaking secrecy. 4) m1 XOR m2, the XOR of the two plaintexts. 5) A fresh nonce (number used once) per message."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reusing a keystream or pad across messages — the single most common stream-cipher failure. Mistake: thinking a stream cipher provides integrity; XOR ciphertext can be flipped bit for bit without detection. Exit: (a) What is (m XOR k) XOR k? (b) What must change for each message when reusing a key? Answers: m; the nonce (so the keystream differs)."}]}'::jsonb, 'XOR is its own inverse: (m XOR k) XOR k = m. That single fact is the heart of every stream cipher.', 13),
  (4, 'Block ciphers (AES) and modes of operation', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe what a block cipher does, explain why AES is a keyed permutation, and choose among modes like ECB, CBC, and CTR for the right reasons. Warm-up: if identical plaintext blocks always produce identical ciphertext blocks, what pattern might leak?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A block cipher takes a fixed-size block (AES uses 128 bits) and a key, and permutes the block invertibly: with the key you can go both ways, without it the map looks random. AES applies several rounds of substitution and mixing; with a 128-, 192-, or 256-bit key it is the workhorse of modern encryption. But a block cipher only handles one block, so a mode of operation extends it to arbitrary length. ECB encrypts each block independently and is unsafe: equal plaintext blocks yield equal ciphertext blocks, leaking structure. CBC XORs each plaintext block with the previous ciphertext block and needs a random initialization vector (IV). CTR turns the block cipher into a stream cipher by encrypting a counter and XORing the result, allowing parallel encryption. The IV or counter must be unique per message."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"ECB leakage: encrypting a bitmap image in ECB leaves the outline visible, because every white block maps to the same ciphertext block — the picture survives encryption. CBC chaining: c0 = E(IV XOR m0), then c1 = E(c0 XOR m1), and so on; each block depends on all previous ones, so identical plaintext blocks now differ. Decryption reverses this: m1 = D(c1) XOR c0. CTR mode: for block i, compute keystream z_i = E(nonce concatenated with counter i), then c_i = m_i XOR z_i; because blocks are independent, you can encrypt or decrypt any block on its own. In every case, reusing an IV or counter with the same key undoes the protection, just as with stream ciphers."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) How many bits are in one AES block? 2) Why is ECB unsafe for structured data? 3) In CBC, what does each ciphertext block depend on? 4) How does CTR mode turn a block cipher into a stream cipher? 5) What must be unique per message in CBC and CTR? Answers: 1) 128 bits. 2) Identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext blocks, leaking patterns. 3) The current plaintext block and all previous ciphertext blocks (via chaining from the IV). 4) It encrypts a counter to make a keystream, then XORs it with the plaintext. 5) The IV (CBC) or the nonce/counter start (CTR)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: using ECB because it is simplest — it leaks. Mistake: reusing an IV or counter, which links messages and can expose plaintext. Exit: (a) What is a mode of operation for? (b) Which common mode should you avoid for structured data? Answers: to encrypt messages longer than one block; ECB."}]}'::jsonb, 'A block cipher encrypts one fixed-size block; a mode of operation is the recipe for chaining blocks to encrypt a whole message.', 14),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Which property does encryption alone NOT guarantee?","options":["confidentiality","integrity","some secrecy of content","obscuring plaintext"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Encryption hides content but does not detect tampering; integrity needs a MAC."},{"prompt":"For m = 1010 and key k = 0110, the ciphertext m XOR k is…","options":["1100","0110","1010","1111"],"answer":0,"explanation":"1010 XOR 0110 = 1100, bit by bit."},{"prompt":"Reusing the same keystream for two messages leaks…","options":["nothing","the XOR of the two plaintexts","the key directly","the message length only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"c1 XOR c2 = m1 XOR m2, canceling the keystream."},{"prompt":"AES encrypts a block of…","options":["64 bits","128 bits","256 bytes","an arbitrary length"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AES uses a fixed 128-bit block regardless of key size."},{"prompt":"ECB mode is considered unsafe because…","options":["it is too slow","identical plaintext blocks map to identical ciphertext blocks","it needs a nonce","it cannot be decrypted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Repeating plaintext blocks reveal structure in the ciphertext."},{"prompt":"CTR mode works by…","options":["chaining ciphertext blocks","encrypting a counter to form a keystream, then XORing","hashing each block","reversing the key"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Encrypting successive counter values yields a keystream XORed with the plaintext."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Message integrity: MACs and hash functions', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain what a cryptographic hash guarantees, describe a MAC, and say why HMAC combines the two. Warm-up: if you post a file''s hash publicly, can an attacker who changes the file also change the posted hash to match?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A cryptographic hash function maps any input to a fixed-length digest (SHA-256 outputs 256 bits). It should be one-way (given a digest you cannot find an input) and collision-resistant (you cannot find two inputs with the same digest). A hash alone gives integrity only if the digest travels over a trusted channel, since anyone can recompute a hash. A message authentication code (MAC) fixes this by mixing in a secret key: the sender computes tag = MAC(key, message) and appends it; the receiver recomputes it and checks for a match. Without the key an attacker cannot produce a valid tag, so a correct tag proves both integrity and authenticity. HMAC is a standard, careful way to build a MAC from a hash function."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Integrity check: a download page lists the SHA-256 digest of a file. You download, hash your copy, and compare. If a single byte differs, the digests differ completely — the avalanche effect. But if an attacker controls the page, they post a matching digest for their altered file, so a bare hash trusts the channel. MAC in action: Alice and Bob share key k. Alice sends (m, t) with t = HMAC(k, m). Bob recomputes HMAC(k, m) and accepts only if it equals t. An attacker who changes m to m'' cannot compute HMAC(k, m'') without k, so the forgery is rejected. Note a subtlety: comparing tags must be done in constant time, or timing differences can leak how many bytes matched."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What two properties should a cryptographic hash have? 2) How many bits does SHA-256 output? 3) Why is a bare hash insufficient over an untrusted channel? 4) What secret ingredient turns integrity into authenticity? 5) Why compare MAC tags in constant time? Answers: 1) One-wayness and collision resistance. 2) 256 bits. 3) Anyone can recompute a hash, so an attacker who alters the message can post a matching digest. 4) A shared secret key (the MAC key). 5) A variable-time compare can leak, through timing, how much of the tag matched, helping forgery."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating a hash as a MAC — a hash has no secret, so it cannot prove authenticity. Mistake: leaking timing when checking tags. Exit: (a) Which primitive proves a message came from someone holding the key? (b) Does a hash function use a secret key? Answers: a MAC; no."}]}'::jsonb, 'A hash gives integrity against accidental change; a MAC adds a secret key so an attacker cannot forge a valid tag.', 13),
  (7, 'Authenticated encryption', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why confidentiality and integrity must be combined, describe encrypt-then-MAC, and name a modern authenticated cipher. Warm-up: if a receiver decrypts attacker-modified ciphertext before checking a tag, what could go wrong?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Encryption without integrity is dangerous: an attacker can flip ciphertext bits, and in a stream or CTR cipher that flips the same plaintext bits, silently altering the message. Authenticated encryption (AE) provides both confidentiality and integrity in one step, and its variant AEAD also authenticates associated data — headers that must be visible but tamper-evident. The recommended construction is encrypt-then-MAC: first encrypt, then compute a MAC over the ciphertext (and any associated data). The receiver verifies the MAC first and only decrypts if it passes, so forged ciphertext never reaches the decryption logic. AES-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305 are widely used AEAD schemes that do this efficiently and are the default in modern protocols."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Bit-flip attack without integrity: suppose CTR-mode ciphertext encodes ''PAY 0100''. Because c = m XOR z, flipping specific ciphertext bits flips the corresponding plaintext bits, and the attacker turns it into ''PAY 9100'' without knowing the key. A MAC over the ciphertext detects the change and the receiver rejects it. Order matters: with encrypt-then-MAC, verification happens on the ciphertext before decryption, so tampered data is discarded early. With the weaker MAC-then-encrypt order, the receiver must decrypt first, which historically enabled padding-oracle attacks that leaked plaintext one byte at a time. AEAD packages the safe order for you: you pass plaintext, a nonce, and associated data, and get ciphertext plus a tag."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What two goals does authenticated encryption combine? 2) In encrypt-then-MAC, what is the MAC computed over? 3) When should the receiver decrypt? 4) What does the ''AD'' in AEAD authenticate? 5) Name one modern AEAD cipher. Answers: 1) Confidentiality and integrity. 2) The ciphertext (plus any associated data). 3) Only after the MAC verifies. 4) Associated data such as headers that are sent in the clear but must not be altered. 5) AES-GCM (or ChaCha20-Poly1305)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: encrypting without authenticating, leaving ciphertext malleable. Mistake: decrypting before verifying the tag, inviting padding-oracle attacks. Exit: (a) Which order is recommended, encrypt-then-MAC or MAC-then-encrypt? (b) Should you decrypt before or after verifying? Answers: encrypt-then-MAC; after."}]}'::jsonb, 'Encrypt-then-MAC: encrypt the plaintext, then MAC the ciphertext. Verify the MAC before you ever decrypt.', 12),
  (8, 'Key exchange: Diffie-Hellman and the discrete log problem', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Run a small Diffie-Hellman exchange by hand and explain what makes it secure. Warm-up: is computing 5^6 mod 23 easy? Is finding x such that 5^x mod 23 = 8 easy?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Diffie-Hellman lets two parties agree on a shared secret over a public channel without meeting first. Everyone knows a large prime p and a base g. Alice picks a secret a and sends A = g^a mod p; Bob picks a secret b and sends B = g^b mod p. Alice computes B^a mod p and Bob computes A^b mod p, and both equal g^(a*b) mod p — the shared secret. An eavesdropper sees p, g, A, and B but must find a from A = g^a mod p, which is the discrete logarithm problem, believed hard for large well-chosen p. Note that plain Diffie-Hellman is unauthenticated: it stops passive eavesdroppers but a man-in-the-middle can impersonate each side, which is why real protocols add signatures or certificates."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Use p = 23 and g = 5. Alice''s secret a = 6: A = 5^6 mod 23 = 8. Bob''s secret b = 15: B = 5^15 mod 23 = 19. Alice computes B^a = 19^6 mod 23 and Bob computes A^b = 8^15 mod 23; both equal g^(a*b) = 5^(90) mod 23. Since 90 mod 22 leaves 2 and 5^2 = 25 = 2 mod 23, the shared secret is 2. The eavesdropper knows 5, 23, 8, and 19 but to recover a must solve 5^a mod 23 = 8, the discrete log — trivial here by trying exponents, but infeasible when p has hundreds of digits. Modern deployments favor elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman, which achieves the same with much smaller keys, and ephemeral keys per session for forward secrecy."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) With p = 23, g = 5, and a = 6, what is A? 2) What quantity do both parties end up computing? 3) What hard problem must an eavesdropper solve? 4) Why is basic Diffie-Hellman vulnerable to a man-in-the-middle? 5) What does using a fresh key per session provide? Answers: 1) 5^6 mod 23 = 8. 2) g^(a*b) mod p, the shared secret. 3) The discrete logarithm problem: find a from g^a mod p. 4) It is unauthenticated, so an attacker can substitute their own A and B to each side. 5) Forward secrecy — past sessions stay safe even if a long-term key later leaks."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking Diffie-Hellman authenticates the parties — it does not without added signatures. Mistake: using a tiny or poorly chosen p, making discrete logs feasible. Exit: (a) What shared value do both parties compute? (b) What problem underlies its security? Answers: g^(a*b) mod p; the discrete logarithm problem."}]}'::jsonb, 'Everything here is modular exponentiation: g^x mod p is easy forward, but recovering x from g^x mod p is believed hard.', 14),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A MAC differs from a plain hash because it…","options":["is shorter","mixes in a secret key","is reversible","needs no computation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The secret key is what an attacker lacks, so only key-holders can forge a valid tag."},{"prompt":"Authenticated encryption provides confidentiality plus…","options":["compression","integrity","faster keys","a public key"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AE combines secrecy with tamper detection in one construction."},{"prompt":"The recommended combination order is…","options":["MAC-then-encrypt","encrypt-then-MAC","hash-then-send","encrypt-then-encrypt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Encrypt-then-MAC lets the receiver reject forged ciphertext before decrypting."},{"prompt":"With p = 23 and g = 5, the value 5^6 mod 23 is…","options":["8","6","15","2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"5^3 = 10 and 5^6 = 100 mod 23 = 8."},{"prompt":"Diffie-Hellman''s security rests on the hardness of…","options":["factoring","the discrete logarithm problem","sorting","hashing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Recovering the secret exponent from g^a mod p is the discrete log problem."},{"prompt":"Plain (unauthenticated) Diffie-Hellman is vulnerable to…","options":["a passive eavesdropper","a man-in-the-middle","a slow network","a weak hash"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without authentication, an active attacker can impersonate each side."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Public-key encryption: RSA and trapdoors', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Generate a tiny RSA key by hand, encrypt and decrypt a small number, and explain the trapdoor. Warm-up: if n = 33, can you find its two prime factors quickly? What if n had 600 digits?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Public-key encryption gives everyone two keys: a public key to encrypt and a private key to decrypt. Anyone can send you a secret message, but only you can read it. RSA builds this from a trapdoor: pick two primes p and q, set n = p*q, and compute phi(n) = (p-1)(q-1). Choose a public exponent e coprime to phi(n), and find the private exponent d with e*d congruent to 1 mod phi(n). Encryption is c = m^e mod n; decryption is m = c^d mod n. The public key is (n, e); the private key is d. Recovering d from the public key essentially requires factoring n into p and q, which is infeasible for large n. In practice we never encrypt raw messages with textbook RSA — we add padding (OAEP) and usually use RSA only to wrap a symmetric key."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Tiny RSA by hand: let p = 3 and q = 11, so n = 33 and phi(n) = 2*10 = 20. Choose e = 3 (gcd(3, 20) = 1). Find d with 3*d congruent to 1 mod 20: d = 7, since 3*7 = 21 = 20 + 1. Public key (33, 3), private key 7. Encrypt m = 4: c = 4^3 mod 33 = 64 mod 33 = 31. Decrypt: m = 31^7 mod 33. Since 31 is congruent to -2 mod 33, we get (-2)^7 = -128, and -128 mod 33 = -128 + 132 = 4 — the original message. The trapdoor: an attacker who could factor 33 = 3*11 would recover phi and then d, but for a 600-digit n no known method factors in reasonable time. That gap is RSA''s security."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) With p = 3, q = 11, what are n and phi(n)? 2) Verify that e = 3 and d = 7 satisfy e*d congruent to 1 mod 20. 3) Encrypt m = 2 with (n, e) = (33, 3). 4) What secret would let an attacker compute d from the public key? 5) Why is textbook RSA not used directly on messages? Answers: 1) n = 33, phi = 20. 2) 3*7 = 21 = 1 mod 20, correct. 3) 2^3 = 8 mod 33 = 8. 4) The factorization of n into p and q (which reveals phi). 5) It is deterministic and malleable; real use adds padding like OAEP and encrypts only a symmetric key."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: computing d modulo n instead of modulo phi(n). Mistake: using unpadded RSA, which is insecure for real messages. Exit: (a) What is the encryption formula in RSA? (b) What hard problem protects the private key? Answers: c = m^e mod n; factoring n."}]}'::jsonb, 'A trapdoor function is easy one way and hard to reverse unless you hold a secret. RSA''s trapdoor is the factorization of n.', 15),
  (11, 'Digital signatures and certificates', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how a signature proves authorship, contrast signing with encrypting, and describe how a certificate binds a key to an identity. Warm-up: to sign a message, which of your two keys do you use — public or private?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A digital signature lets anyone verify that a message came from a specific private-key holder and was not altered. The signer hashes the message and transforms the digest with their private key; a verifier uses the matching public key to check it. Only the private-key holder could produce a signature that the public key accepts, giving authenticity and integrity, and non-repudiation — the signer cannot later deny it. But a public key is just a number; how do you know it belongs to a given website or person? A certificate answers this: a trusted certificate authority (CA) signs a statement binding an identity to a public key. Your browser trusts a set of root CAs, and a chain of signatures links a site''s certificate up to a trusted root."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Signing versus encrypting mirror each other. To send Bob a secret, you encrypt with Bob''s public key so only Bob''s private key decrypts. To prove you wrote a message, you sign with your private key so anyone with your public key can verify. Signing the hash, not the whole message, keeps signatures short and fast — you sign the fixed-size digest. Certificate chain: a browser visits a bank; the bank presents a certificate saying ''this public key belongs to bank.example'', signed by a CA. The browser checks the CA''s signature using the CA''s public key, which is itself vouched for by a root certificate shipped with the browser. If any signature in the chain fails, or a certificate is expired or revoked, the browser refuses. This is how a public key gets tied to a real identity."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which key signs a message? 2) Which key verifies the signature? 3) What three guarantees does a signature give? 4) What does a certificate authority actually sign? 5) Why sign a hash rather than the whole message? Answers: 1) The sender''s private key. 2) The sender''s public key. 3) Authenticity, integrity, and non-repudiation. 4) A binding between an identity and a public key. 5) The digest is small and fixed-size, so signing is fast and the signature is compact."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: swapping the keys — signing with the public key or verifying with the private key. Mistake: trusting a certificate without checking its chain to a trusted root. Exit: (a) Which key verifies a signature? (b) What does a certificate bind together? Answers: the signer''s public key; an identity and a public key."}]}'::jsonb, 'Encryption uses the receiver''s public key; a signature uses the sender''s private key. They are mirror images.', 13),
  (12, 'How HTTPS works: the TLS handshake', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Trace a TLS handshake step by step and explain how it delivers confidentiality, integrity, and server authenticity together. Warm-up: why not just use RSA or Diffie-Hellman for the whole connection instead of switching to a symmetric cipher?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"HTTPS is HTTP carried inside TLS, the protocol that secures most web traffic. TLS solves a bootstrapping problem: two strangers must agree on keys and be sure of who they are talking to. The handshake uses public-key cryptography to authenticate and to establish a shared secret, then switches to fast symmetric authenticated encryption for the actual data. Public-key operations are slow, so they are used only briefly to set up per-session symmetric keys — this hybrid design gives both trust and speed. Modern TLS uses ephemeral Diffie-Hellman for the key exchange so that each session''s keys vanish afterward, providing forward secrecy: even if the server''s long-term private key later leaks, past recorded sessions stay unreadable."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A simplified TLS 1.3 handshake: (1) the client sends a hello with supported ciphers and its ephemeral Diffie-Hellman public value. (2) The server replies with its own ephemeral value, its certificate, and a signature over the handshake. (3) The client verifies the certificate chain up to a trusted root and checks the signature, authenticating the server. (4) Both sides combine the Diffie-Hellman values into the same shared secret and derive symmetric keys from it. (5) From then on, every record is protected with an AEAD cipher such as AES-GCM, giving confidentiality and integrity. The certificate proves you are really talking to bank.example; the ephemeral key exchange means the session key is never sent on the wire; the AEAD layer stops both eavesdropping and tampering."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does HTTPS stand for at heart — HTTP over what? 2) Why does TLS switch to a symmetric cipher after the handshake? 3) What authenticates the server during the handshake? 4) What property does ephemeral Diffie-Hellman provide? 5) Which class of cipher protects the application data? Answers: 1) HTTP over TLS. 2) Symmetric encryption is much faster than public-key operations for bulk data. 3) Its certificate (checked to a trusted root) plus a signature over the handshake. 4) Forward secrecy. 5) An AEAD cipher such as AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking public-key crypto encrypts the whole session — it only sets up the symmetric keys. Mistake: assuming encryption alone authenticates the server; the certificate does that. Exit: (a) What provides forward secrecy in modern TLS? (b) What secures the bulk data after the handshake? Answers: ephemeral Diffie-Hellman; a symmetric AEAD cipher."}]}'::jsonb, 'TLS combines the tools you have met: public-key auth and key exchange to set up, then fast symmetric AEAD to carry the data.', 14),
  (13, 'Checkpoint 3', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"In RSA with p = 3, q = 11, the value phi(n) is…","options":["33","20","14","30"],"answer":1,"explanation":"phi(n) = (p-1)(q-1) = 2*10 = 20."},{"prompt":"RSA encryption of message m uses the formula…","options":["c = m + e mod n","c = m^e mod n","c = e^m mod n","c = m XOR e"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ciphertext is c = m^e mod n; decryption is m = c^d mod n."},{"prompt":"To create a digital signature, the sender uses their…","options":["public key","private key","shared symmetric key","certificate authority''s key"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only the private-key holder can produce a signature the public key accepts."},{"prompt":"A certificate authority signs a binding between…","options":["two ciphertexts","an identity and a public key","a nonce and a counter","two primes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The CA vouches that a public key belongs to a named identity."},{"prompt":"HTTPS is HTTP running over…","options":["FTP","TLS","SSH","DNS"],"answer":1,"explanation":"TLS provides the encryption, integrity, and server authentication for HTTPS."},{"prompt":"Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman in TLS provides…","options":["compression","forward secrecy","faster hashing","a longer certificate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Per-session keys mean a later leak of the long-term key does not expose past sessions."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (14, 'Applied cryptography: password hashing, randomness, and common pitfalls', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Store passwords the right way, tell a secure random source from a predictable one, and spot recurring implementation traps. Warm-up: if two users pick the same password, why should their stored hashes still look different?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Applied cryptography fails more often at the edges than in the math. Passwords should never be stored in plaintext or under a plain fast hash; use a deliberately slow, salted password-hashing function like bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2. A salt is a unique random value per user, stored alongside the hash, so identical passwords yield different hashes and precomputed rainbow tables become useless. Randomness matters everywhere: keys, nonces, IVs, and salts must come from a cryptographically secure random generator, not a predictable one seeded by the clock. And several pitfalls recur: reusing a nonce, comparing secrets in variable time, rolling your own cipher, or ignoring authentication. The rule of thumb is to use well-reviewed libraries and standard constructions rather than inventing your own."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Salting: users Ana and Ben both choose ''sunshine''. Without a salt, both rows store the same hash, so cracking one cracks both and a rainbow table maps it instantly. With per-user salts s1 and s2, you store hash(salt, password) and the salts differ, so the stored values differ and precomputation fails. Slow by design: a fast hash like SHA-256 lets an attacker try billions of guesses per second; Argon2 is tuned to take, say, a fraction of a second per guess and to use memory, cutting an offline attacker''s rate enormously. Bad randomness: a predictable generator seeded from the current time produces guessable keys and nonces; a secure generator (the operating system''s cryptographic source) does not. Nonce reuse: encrypting two messages with AES-GCM under the same key and nonce can leak plaintext and even the authentication key — never repeat a nonce."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Why store passwords with a slow function instead of a fast one? 2) What does a salt prevent? 3) Where must cryptographically secure randomness be used? 4) What can go wrong if you reuse a nonce with AES-GCM? 5) What is the safest general strategy for building crypto? Answers: 1) Slowness limits how many guesses an offline attacker can try per second. 2) It stops rainbow-table precomputation and hides that two users share a password. 3) For keys, nonces, IVs, and salts. 4) It can leak plaintext and compromise the authentication, breaking security. 5) Use standard, well-reviewed libraries and constructions instead of inventing your own."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: hashing passwords with a fast general-purpose hash, or with no salt. Mistake: sourcing keys or nonces from a predictable generator. Exit: (a) Name a suitable password-hashing function. (b) Why must a salt be unique per user? Answers: bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2; so identical passwords produce different stored hashes and precomputation fails."}]}'::jsonb, 'Fast hashes are great for integrity but terrible for passwords — for passwords you deliberately want slowness.', 13),
  (15, 'Capstone: design a secure messaging scheme', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"The task","text":"Design, in your own words, an end-to-end scheme by which Alice and Bob exchange messages so that an active network attacker cannot read or tamper with them, and each is sure of the other''s identity. Do not implement it — specify which cryptographic tool handles each goal and justify every choice using the ideas from this course."},{"heading":"The building blocks to assemble","text":"1) Identity and trust: how does each party obtain and verify the other''s public key — for example via certificates signed by a trusted authority? 2) Key agreement: use ephemeral Diffie-Hellman so each session has fresh keys and enjoys forward secrecy, and authenticate the exchange with signatures so a man-in-the-middle cannot substitute keys. 3) Bulk protection: derive symmetric keys from the shared secret and protect every message with an AEAD cipher such as AES-GCM, using a unique nonce per message. 4) Integrity and authenticity: rely on the AEAD tag (or an encrypt-then-MAC construction) so tampering is rejected before decryption. 5) Operational care: use a secure random generator for keys and nonces, never reuse a nonce, and compare tags in constant time."},{"heading":"A worked design sketch","text":"Setup: Alice and Bob each hold a long-term signing key whose public half is certified. Handshake: they run ephemeral Diffie-Hellman, and each signs their ephemeral public value with their long-term key, so both authenticate the exchange and defeat a man-in-the-middle; they then derive session keys from the shared secret. Messaging: each message is sealed with AES-GCM under the session key and a fresh nonce, giving confidentiality and integrity at once; the receiver verifies the tag before decrypting and discards anything that fails. Properties achieved: confidentiality (AEAD encryption), integrity and authenticity (AEAD tags plus authenticated handshake), identity assurance (certificates and signatures), and forward secrecy (ephemeral keys). Use this as a model of the reasoning expected — trace each of the three goals to a specific mechanism."},{"heading":"Self-check rubric","text":"Check yes to each: every message''s confidentiality is tied to a named cipher; integrity and authenticity are tied to a MAC or AEAD tag; identities are verified through certificates or pre-shared public keys; the key agreement is authenticated so a man-in-the-middle fails; ephemeral keys give forward secrecy; and nonces are unique with randomness from a secure source. If all six hold, your design covers confidentiality, integrity, and authenticity end to end without reinventing primitives."}]}'::jsonb, 'A good design names, for every message, which tool provides confidentiality, which provides integrity and authenticity, and how the keys were agreed.', 15),
  (16, 'Comprehensive final quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Which set names cryptography''s three core goals?","options":["speed, size, cost","confidentiality, integrity, authenticity","encoding, hashing, salting","backup, logging, auditing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Secrecy, tamper-detection, and provable origin are the three pillars."},{"prompt":"Kerckhoffs''s principle says security should rest on…","options":["hiding the algorithm","the secret key alone","a fast network","a long message"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Assume the attacker knows the design; only the key stays secret."},{"prompt":"Reusing a stream-cipher keystream across two messages leaks…","options":["nothing useful","the XOR of the two plaintexts","the key itself","only the lengths"],"answer":1,"explanation":"c1 XOR c2 = m1 XOR m2 cancels the keystream."},{"prompt":"ECB mode is unsafe because…","options":["it is too slow","identical plaintext blocks yield identical ciphertext blocks","it lacks a key","it cannot decrypt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Repeating blocks reveal structure in the ciphertext."},{"prompt":"The difference between a hash and a MAC is that a MAC…","options":["is reversible","includes a secret key","is always shorter","needs no computation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The secret key is what lets a MAC prove authenticity, not just integrity."},{"prompt":"The recommended way to combine encryption and a MAC is…","options":["MAC-then-encrypt","encrypt-then-MAC","encrypt only","MAC only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Encrypt-then-MAC verifies ciphertext before any decryption."},{"prompt":"Diffie-Hellman lets two parties compute the shared value…","options":["g + a + b mod p","g^(a*b) mod p","a XOR b","g^a + g^b mod p"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both sides reach g^(a*b) mod p from the exchanged public values."},{"prompt":"RSA''s private key is protected by the hardness of…","options":["sorting","factoring n = p*q","hashing","the discrete log"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Recovering d essentially requires factoring the modulus n."},{"prompt":"With p = 3, q = 11, e = 3, the private exponent d satisfying 3d = 1 mod 20 is…","options":["5","7","9","11"],"answer":1,"explanation":"3*7 = 21 = 20 + 1, so d = 7 mod 20."},{"prompt":"A digital signature is created with the sender''s…","options":["public key","private key","shared key","certificate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only the private-key holder can produce a verifiable signature."},{"prompt":"In TLS, public-key cryptography is used mainly to…","options":["encrypt all application data","authenticate and set up symmetric session keys","compress traffic","store passwords"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The slow public-key step bootstraps fast symmetric encryption for the session."},{"prompt":"The safest way to store user passwords is…","options":["plaintext","a fast unsalted hash","a slow salted function like Argon2","reversible encryption with a shared key"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A slow, salted password-hashing function resists offline guessing and precomputation."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0002-0000-4000-8000-00000000001e' and l.position = v.pos
);
